WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.780
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, Episode 49. It's May 25th,

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:11.160
2026. We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.200
So imagine you go to a doctor and they tell you that you have a serious eye condition called

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:23.960
Bixinomania. The only problem is that Bixinomania does not exist at all. A researcher in Sweden made

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:29.600
the whole thing up, complete with fake research papers that sounded official. They posted these papers

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.860
online just to see what would happen next. What happened is that major AI chatbots from Open AI,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:42.620
Google, and Microsoft started repeating the fake details as if they were real. The chatbots gave

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.520
users made up statistics about the condition and even suggested seeing a doctor for it.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.880
Think of the internet like a giant library where anyone can add a book without any library and

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.880
checking whether the story inside is true. The AI systems are like very fast readers who absorb

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.520
every single page and then answer questions based on whatever they just read. They do not have a built-in

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.280
filter that says, this book sounds ridiculous so we should ignore it. This experiment matters

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:18.200
because over 40 million people every day ask tools like chat GPT health questions. When the answer

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.900
sound confident it is easy to treat them as fact instead of a starting point. If you are a student

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:29.620
working on a project or a parent checking symptoms, you could end up with advice that is completely wrong.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.400
The false information can even find its way into real medical journals before anyone notices the

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.980
problem. For you personally, this means any medical answer from an AI should be checked against

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:46.760
trusted sources like the Centers for Disease Control or Mayo Clinic. You can try this right now by

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:53.320
asking chat GPT or Gemini a simple health question you already know the answer to. Then compare what the

00:01:53.320 --> 00:02:00.320
AI says against an official site to see where the details match or fall apart. Now for my favorite part

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:06.580
of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood. The question everyone asks after stories like

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.840
this is why the AI repeats nonsense with so much confidence. Think of it like a game of telephone

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.700
played across millions of pages of text scraped from the internet. One person starts a silly rumor as a

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:25.560
joke and by the time it travels around the circle, it sounds like established fact. The AI reads

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.240
everything without pausing to ask whether the original source was a joke or a real study.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:37.180
When the fake papers about Bixen, Emania appear near real scientific text, the model simply blended

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:42.700
the patterns together. Next, it adds extra details that sound scientific because those details

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:48.620
happen to sit near the made up information. And that is basically how the rumor mill works inside

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.960
these systems. The model treats every piece of text the same way, whether it came from a peer-reviewed

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.960
journal or a made up prank. So next time someone says the AI hallucinated, you can picture the world's

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:07.800
fastest rumor mill that never learned to fact check. And that is basically how this kind of misinformation

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:14.680
spreads through AI. Not so scary once you see the steps, right? Now let us look at some cool stuff you can

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:21.080
actually try. One interesting update involves quantum computers helping regular AI models get smarter.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.780
Scientists ran an AI on an IBM quantum computer and found it answered certain questions correctly

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.400
when the normal version got them wrong. The improvement came from adding only a small number of

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.560
extra settings instead of rebuilding the entire system. Think of it like borrowing a special super-powered

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:44.680
calculator for just the hardest part of a math problem rather than buying a whole new computer.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.400
This hints that future AI might improve by teaming up with quantum machines instead of simply growing

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:57.140
larger and larger. Another story that caught my eye involves robots working without breaks.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.800
Figure AI streamed their robots sorting packages in a warehouse nonstop for eight full days.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.600
The live stream showed steady, practical movements instead of short flashy demos that last

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.920
only a few minutes. Picture a team of tireless workers who never need lunch, sleep, or a weekend off,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.940
and you start to see why this matters. It shows robots moving from science experiments toward tools

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:28.060
that could one day help in factories, stores, or even homes. If you want to explore this kind of

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:34.580
robot work yourself, search for Figure AI on YouTube and watch the warehouse live stream replay.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.960
You will see the same steady motion that ran for over a week without human intervention.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:46.100
Here are a couple of quick bits to finish things off. The quantum experiment shows that future AI

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:52.440
improvements might come from clever partnerships rather than sheer size. That is a hopeful sign

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.080
because building ever larger models takes enormous amounts of energy and money.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.160
The robot live stream reminds us that practical, boring reliability is often more important than flashy

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:10.400
tricks. Watching machines handle real repetitive tasks for days on end gives a clearer picture

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:17.140
of where the technology is heading. That is it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:22.920
where you are right now. If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:29.020
how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:36.440
then listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network, links in the show notes. This episode used AI voice synthesis

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.220
of my voice and tutorial selection and analysis are my own.
